\chapter{Canonical control, moves, and verified values}
\label{ch:78}
\index{Canonical control, moves, and verified values}
\index{HIR!independent verification}

Canonicalization lowers high-level control and match constructs to typed regions, branches, slots, projections, and exits. Stable lexical identities let an independent verifier check where a break targets, which paths initialize a result slot, whether a projection is dominated by its tag, and whether a move or loan is valid along every path. Backend input is rebuilt after specialization where identities need recomputing.

\section{Verify a rejected candidate}
For a sum projection, build an invalid test representation in which the projection is moved outside the tag-guarded branch. The corresponding source pattern can still look well formed, so test the bad data directly at verifier ingress. For ownership, attempt to use a consumed binding along only one predecessor and then read it after the join. These cases check path-sensitive obligations after source syntax is erased. Public PPX output cannot publish these private region or proof constructs on the compiler's behalf.

\section{Why a join needs two initialization sets}
Imagine a canonical region declaring a result slot before an if. The
true arm initializes the slot with seven; the false arm reaches the join
without writing it. At the join ask two questions. Has the slot
\emph{definitely} been initialized? No: the false edge witnesses the
contrary. Has it \emph{possibly} been initialized? Yes: the true edge
witnesses that. The value verifier intersects definite-initialization sets
but unions possible-initialization sets when combining path states.

Try two tempting repairs after the join. Reading the slot fails because
definite initialization is missing. Adding an unconditional initialization
there also fails: on the true path it would initialize the same storage
twice. Initializing it only in the false arm makes both paths arrive with
one value and permits the read. Alternatively, if the false arm returns
from the function, it no longer supplies an uninitialized path to the
join. Count arriving edges, not just branches in the original text.

The distinction matters on cycles as well. Enclosing storage first
initialized on a repeatable loop backedge could be initialized again on
the next iteration. A single traversal of a drawn loop that happens to
exit after one trip would miss this violation. Use one verifier fixture
with mutually exclusive initializing arms and another with the write on
the backedge. They contain the same typed write operation, yet one makes
a value available and the other fails the storage discipline. A type
annotation on the slot does not change either conclusion. Slot identity
matters too: a write to a nested declaration of the same name is no
initialization of the enclosing result.

